nding 1: `role=\"toolbar\"` without proper keyboard interaction pattern (4.1.2)**\n\nThe WAI-ARIA APG toolbar pattern specifies that when a toolbar receives focus, arrow keys should move between controls within the toolbar, and Tab moves focus out of the toolbar. In this implementation, all controls are simply in normal tab order - there's no special arrow-key navigation within the toolbar. This means:\n1. The `role=\"toolbar\"` is applied but its expected keyboard interaction pattern isn't implemented\n2. Screen reader users might expect arrow key navigation between controls when they encounter a toolbar role\n\nThis could be either:\n- A finding that the toolbar role shouldn't be used (remove it), OR\n- A finding that proper toolbar keyboard interactions should be added\n\nPer WCAG 4.1.2, custom widgets must use correct ARIA roles AND have appropriate behavior. Using `role=\"toolbar\"` without implementing its expected keyboard interaction is a mismatch between role and behavior. This would be a MAJOR finding.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Is the toolbar role really causing harm here? The controls are all natively focusable and operable via keyboard (Tab to reach them). The issue is that screen readers might announce \"toolbar\" and then users might try arrow keys expecting to navigate within it, but instead Tab moves between elements normally. This creates a mismatch between expected and actual behavior.\n\nThe fix would be either:\n1. Remove `role=\"toolbar\"` since the controls work fine in normal tab order\n2. Implement proper toolbar keyboard interactions (arrow key navigation)\n\nI think this is a MAJOR finding because it's a role-behavior mismatch that could confuse screen reader users.\n\n**Finding 2: Time display with `aria-live=\"off\"` (4.1.3)**\n\nThe time display `<span className=\"time-display\" aria-live=\"off\">` shows the current playback position. With `aria-live=\"off\"`, changes to this text are not announced by screen readers. For a media player, users might want to know when the video has advanced or reached certain points. However, announcing every second of playback time would be very noisy and potentially harmful for screen reader users.\n\nActually, let me think about what 4.1.3 says: \"Status messages can be programmatically determined through an assertion rather than being presented to AT.\" The key question is whether the time display constitutes a \"status message\" that should be announced. In media players, it's common practice NOT to announce every second of playback time because it would create excessive announcements. However, certain events (like reaching the end of the video) might warrant announcement.\n\nI think this is more of an ENHANCEMENT or MINOR finding rather than a MAJOR one. The current implementation doesn't actively harm users - it just doesn't announce time changes, which is actually reasonable behavior for a media player. But there's no mechanism to announce important status changes (like video ending).\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully: the `aria-live=\"off\"` on the time display means that even if a screen reader user wants to know the current position, they can't get it announced automatically. They'd have to navigate to the element manually. This is actually acceptable behavior for media players - most don't announce every second of playback time.\n\nBut wait - there's no `aria-live` region at all for important status changes like \"video ended\" or \"buffering.\" That could be a MINOR finding. Let me check if this component has any such announcements... No, it doesn't. But the checklist item is about whether status messages use aria-live appropriately, and since there are no critical status messages being generated (like errors), I'll consider this as potentially N/A or a minor enhancement suggestion.\n\nLet me reconsider: The time display updates dynamically but with `aria-live=\"off\"`. This means screen reader users won't hear time changes. Is this a problem? For most media players, not announcing every second of playback is actually preferred behavior. However, some implementations use `aria-live=\"polite\"` to announce periodic updates (like every 30 seconds or at chapter markers). The absence of any live region for important events could be flagged as MINOR.\n\nActually, I think the more significant issue with the time display is that it's not properly associated with anything meaningful for screen readers beyond its aria-label on the seek slider. Let me focus on what's actually wrong here.\n\n**Finding 3: Poster image without alternative (1.1.1)**\n\nThe video has `poster=\"/img/tutorial-poster.jpg\"`. Per WCAG 1.1.1, if the poster provides information that isn't available elsewhere, it should have an alternative. However, since the video content is described in captions and transcript, the poster is likely decorative. But there's no explicit indication of this. The `<video>` element doesn't have a `title` or `aria-label` attribute to describe its purpose beyond what's provided by surrounding context (the h2 heading).\n\nActually, looking at this more carefully:\n- The video element itself has no accessible name. It relies on the surrounding `<h2>Tutorial: Getting Started</h2>` for context. \n- Per WCAG 4.1.2, UI components should have an accessible name that describes their purpose. A video player without a programmatic label might not be properly identified by screen readers.\n\nWait, but the video has `tabIndex={-1}` so it's not focusable directly - users interact with it through the custom controls in the toolbar. The play button has `aria-label=\"Play video\"` which provides context. So the video element itself doesn't need an accessible name because it's never focused or announced independently.\n\nHmm, but some screen readers might still announce the video element when navigating by landmarks or elements. Let me check: the `<section>` with `aria-label=\"Video player\"` wraps everything, so that should provide context for the group of controls. The individual controls have their own labels. I think this is actually fine.\n\nLet me reconsider the poster issue. Per WCAG 1.1.1: \"Non-text content... that is presented to the user at a time when the user can choose between display and not display\" - posters are shown before video plays, so they're visible content. If the poster conveys meaningful information (like a scene from the video), it should have an alternative. But if it's just a branded placeholder, it might be decorative.\n\nSince we can't determine what's in the poster image, and since the video has captions and transcript that describe its content, I'll consider this as potentially N/A or requiring human verification. Actually, per WCAG 1.1.1, the best practice is to provide a `title` attribute on the video element if the poster conveys information not available elsewhere. But many implementations don't do this for posters since they're typically just visual placeholders.\n\nI think I'll note this as something that could be improved but isn't necessarily a violation. Let me focus on clearer findings.\n\n**Finding 4: Seek slider `onChange` vs `onInput`**\n\nThe seek slider uses `onChange={handleSeek}`. For range inputs, `onChange` fires when the value is committed (after the user releases the mouse or stops dragging). However, for better accessibility and usability, especially with screen readers that might use arrow keys to adjust the slider, `onChange` should work fine since it fires on each change. But some implementations prefer `onInput` for real-time feedback during dragging. This isn't really an accessibility issue per se - both events are accessible via keyboard (arrow keys trigger onChange).\n\nActually, in React, `onChange` on range inputs behaves like `onInput` in native HTML - it fires continuously as the value changes. So this is fine from an accessibility perspective.\n\n**Finding 5: Time display not announced to screen readers**\n\nThe `<span className=\"time-display\" aria-live=\"off\">` shows current/duration time but doesn't announce updates. For a media player, some users might want periodic announcements of playback position. However, announcing every second would be excessive. The common pattern is either:\n1. Don't announce (current behavior) - acceptable for most cases\n2. Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` with throttled updates\n\nI think the current implementation is acceptable but could be enhanced. This might be a MINOR finding or ENHANCEMENT.\n\nLet me also check if there are any issues I'm missing:\n\n**Finding 6: No `title` attribute on video element for poster description (1.1.1)**\n\nThe video has a poster image but no title attribute to describe it. If the poster conveys meaningful information, this should be addressed. However, since we can't determine what's in the poster and the video content is described elsewhere, I'll note this as requiring human verification or consider it N/A.\n\nActually wait - let me re-read the WCAG 1.1.1 more carefully:\n\"For non-text content that is rendered in a way that provides information to the user when the non-text content is not rendered, provide a text alternative...\"\n\nThe poster IS shown to users (it's visible before play). If it conveys meaningful info, it needs an alt. But video posters are typically just visual representations of the video content, which is already described by captions and transcript. I'll consider this as potentially N/A since the information is available elsewhere.\n\nLet me also look at whether there's a missing accessible name for the video element itself:\n\nThe `<video>` element has no `aria-label`, `title`, or associated label. Per WCAG 4.1.2, it should have an accessible name. However, since the video isn't focusable (tabIndex=-1) and is controlled through labeled buttons in a toolbar with aria-label=\"Video controls\", the context is provided by surrounding elements. The `<section>` has `aria-label=\"Video player\"` which