ng sets it to 'disabled'. When false, clicking sets it to 'showing'. This logic appears correct. \u2713\n\nBut wait - there's a potential issue with the initial state of text tracks. The `<track>` element has a `default` attribute which means the browser should show captions by default when the video loads. However, JavaScript manipulation of `textTracks[i].mode` might not work immediately because text tracks aren't available until metadata is loaded. Let me check...\n\nActually, in React with refs, `videoRef.current.textTracks` would be empty if called before the video's metadata has loaded. But since this function is only called on user click (not during initialization), the tracks should be available by then. So this is fine from a functional perspective. \u2713\n\nNow let me also check: does the component handle the case where text tracks might not be loaded yet? The `toggleCaptions` function accesses `videoRef.current.textTracks` directly without checking if it exists or has items. If called before metadata loads, it would iterate over an empty list (no error). This is acceptable behavior - no crash, just no effect until tracks are available.\n\nLet me also check for any missing ARIA attributes:\n- The seek slider doesn't have `aria-valuenow` explicitly set, but since it's a native range input with `value={currentTime}`, the browser provides this automatically. \u2713\n- Same for volume slider - native range input with value attribute. \u2713\n- The time display has `aria-live=\"off\"` which is explicit (though \"off\" is the default). This prevents announcements of time changes, which as discussed above might be acceptable but could be enhanced.\n\nOK wait, I need to also check: does the component have any issues with focus management? Let me look at the controls again:\n- Play/pause button - focusable \u2713\n- Seek slider (input type=\"range\") - natively focusable \u2713  \n- Time display span - not focusable (not interactive) \u2713\n- Volume slider (input