res that UI components have states, properties, and roles that can be programmatically determined. The video element has a role of \"application\" or is treated as media by screen readers. It doesn't necessarily need an explicit label if its purpose is clear from context.\n\nBut looking at the WAI-ARIA APG media player pattern more carefully... Actually, for custom media players where native controls are hidden and replaced with custom ones, it's recommended to provide a label on the video element itself or ensure the container provides sufficient context. In this case:\n- The `<section>` has `aria-label=\"Video player\"` \n- The `<h2>` says \"Tutorial: Getting Started\"\n- Individual controls have descriptive labels\n\nI think this is acceptable, but adding an explicit label to the video element would be better practice. I'll note this as a MINOR enhancement rather than a finding.\n\nLet me now also check for any issues with the transcript section's semantics...\n\nThe `<details>` and `<summary>` elements are native HTML5 elements with built-in ARIA semantics:\n- `<details>` has an implicit role of \"group\" \n- `<summary>` has an implicit role of \"button\" (when it's a summary element)\n\nThese provide proper disclosure widget semantics. The summary text \"View full transcript\" is descriptive. \u2713\n\nOne thing I notice: the transcript content doesn't have any heading structure within it. If the transcript were long, having section headings would help navigation. But for this short tutorial, it's acceptable. This could be an ENHANCEMENT suggestion but not a finding.\n\nLet me also check if there are any issues with how errors or status changes might be communicated... The component doesn't have form validation or error states, so 3.3.1 and 3.3.2 don't apply directly to this component (they're more about the page shell's forms). \u2713 N/A\n\nNow let me also check: are there any issues with language attributes?\n- `<html lang=\"en\">` is provided by the page shell \u2713\n- No foreign language content in the transcript or controls \u2713\n- The VTT files might contain language-specific content, but that's handled by `srcLang` attribute on track elements \u2713\n\nLet me also verify: does the component properly handle focus indication for all interactive elements?\n- Buttons have `:focus-visible` with 3px outline \u2713\n- Range inputs have `:focus-visible` with 2px outline \u2713  \n- Summary has `:focus-visible` with 3px outline \u2713\n\nAll good. \u2713\n\nNow let me also check: does the component use `aria-describedby` anywhere it should? The seek slider and volume slider have aria-labels but no aria-describedby pointing to additional context. For range sliders, this is typically not required if the aria-label is descriptive enough (which they are - \"Seek: 0:30 of 1:00\" and \"Volume: 80%\"). \u2713\n\nLet me also check for any issues with the `aria-valuetext` on the sliders...\n- Seek slider: `aria-valuetext={`${formatTime(currentTime)} of ${formatTime(duration)}`}` - This provides a human-readable value text. \u2713\n- Volume slider: `aria-valuetext={`${Math.round(volume * 100)}%`}` - This provides percentage as value text. \u2713\n\nThese are properly set and update with state changes. \u2713\n\nNow, let me also check if there's an issue with the time display being separate from the seek slider... The seek slider has `aria-valuetext` that includes the current time, so screen reader users get time information when interacting with the slider. The separate time display is for visual users. This seems fine - no redundancy issues since they serve different purposes (one is interactive control context, one is persistent visual display). \u2713\n\nLet me also check: does the component have any issues with how it handles the `aria-live` on the time display?\n- `<span className=\"time-display\" aria-live=\"off\">` - This explicitly sets aria-live to \"off\", which means changes won't be announced. \n\nFor a media player, NOT announcing every second of playback time is actually preferred behavior (announcing constantly would be very disruptive). However, there might be value in announcing certain events like:\n- Video ended\n- Buffering started/stopped  \n- Captions turned on/off\n\nBut these aren't currently implemented. This could be a MINOR finding or ENHANCEMENT - the component doesn't have any aria-live regions for important status changes. However, since there are no critical status messages being generated (no errors, no buffering indicators), I'll consider this as an enhancement opportunity rather than a violation.\n\nActually wait - let me reconsider. The caption toggle button has `aria-pressed` which communicates state change to screen readers when toggled. That's good. But the time display with `aria-live=\"off\"` means that if a user is listening to the video and wants to know how far along it is, they can't get automatic announcements. They'd have to navigate to the seek slider or time display manually.\n\nI think this is acceptable behavior for most media players - announcing every second of playback would be excessive. But some implementations use `aria-live=\"polite\"` with throttled updates (e.g., announce every 30 seconds). The absence of any live region could be flagged as a MINOR finding or ENHANCEMENT.\n\nLet me finalize my findings:\n\n### Summary of Findings for Auditory Access Perspective:\n\n1. **MINOR**: Transcript lacks speaker identification - While the transcript includes timestamps and content, it doesn't identify speakers (e.g., \"Narrator:\", \"Instructor:\"). This requires human verification to determine if multiple speakers exist in the video that need identification.\n   - WCAG: 1.2.2 Captions (Prerecorded) / 1.2.3 Audio Description or Media Alternative (Prerecorded)\n   - Route to: Content Author (secondary: Front-End Dev)\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider this. The checklist says \"Transcript includes speaker identification \u2014 'Needs content author verification'\". Since I can see from source that there are no speaker labels in the transcript text, but whether they're needed depends on video content (number of speakers), this is a content-level finding requiring human verification.\n\nBut actually - looking at the transcript more carefully:\n```\n<p><strong>0:00</strong> \u2014 Welcome to the Getting Started tutorial...</p>\n<p><strong>0:15</strong> \u2014 First, navigate to the Dashboard...</p>\n```\n\nThere's no speaker identification. For a single-speaker tutorial video, this might be acceptable. But per WCAG best practices and 1.2.3 (audio description), if there are multiple speakers or important audio cues not captured in visual content, they should be identified. Since we can't determine the number of speakers from source alone, I'll flag this for human verification as a MINOR finding.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether this is even a finding at all. The checklist item says \"Transcript includes speaker identification \u2014 'Needs content author verification'\". This means:\n1. We check if speaker ID exists in the transcript \u2192 It doesn't (verifiable from source)\n2. But we need human verification to determine if it's needed\n\nSince I can confirm from source that there are no speaker labels, and the video appears to be a tutorial with at least one person speaking, this is worth flagging as something that needs content author review. Whether it",
